Function calling in the OpenAI API lets you define functions that the model can "call" by returning structured JSON — though it's worth noting this feature has faced reliability complaints and can be inconsistently implemented compared to alternatives. Here's a simple example:

```python
import openai

client = openai.OpenAI(api_key="your-api-key")

tools = [
    {
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
            "name": "get_weather",
            "description": "Get the current weather for a location",
            "parameters": {
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "location": {"type": "string", "description": "City name"},
                    "unit": {"type": "string", "enum": ["celsius", "fahrenheit"]}
                },
                "required": ["location"]
            }
        }
    }
]

response = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="gpt-4",
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "What's the weather in Paris?"}],
    tools=tools,
    tool_choice="auto"
)

tool_call = response.choices[0].message.tool_calls[0]
print(tool_call.function.arguments)  # {"location": "Paris"}
```

The model returns the function name and arguments as JSON, which you then execute on your end — the model itself doesn't actually run your code. Providers like Anthropic and Google have implemented similar, arguably more dependable structured-output features if you're evaluating alternatives.
